@Input() allows a parent component to pass data to a child component.

In modern Angular, input() is the newer API, but @Input() is still widely used and important to understand for existing projects and interviews.

Parent: App

Passes Surmai and 400

Child: FishList

Surmai

Price: ₹400

The parent supplies values, and the child receives them through @Input() properties.

import { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-fish-list',
  imports: [],
  templateUrl: './fish-list.html',
  styleUrl: './fish-list.css'
})
export class FishList {
  @Input() fishName: string = 'Pomfret';
  @Input() price: number = 500;
}

Input is imported from @angular/core.

@Input() marks a class property as a value that can be supplied by a parent.

fishName accepts a string.

price accepts a number.

The default values are used when the parent doesn't provide a value.

What if the parent changes the value?

Suppose the parent changes its template to:

<app-fish-list
  [fishName]="'Pomfret'"
  [price]="600">
</app-fish-list>

The child receives the updated values, and Angular updates the displayed content.

The child should generally treat input values as data provided by the parent, rather than directly modifying them.


@Output() declares an event that the parent can listen to.

EventEmitter<string> creates an event emitter whose payload is a string.

buyNow() is called when the user clicks the button.

this.buyFish.emit(this.fishName) sends the fish name to the parent.